**Reasoning:** The function is small and self-contained but requires careful boundary-condition logic (distance/time thresholds, which fields survive a merge) that benefits from a model above Economy tier without needing Premium-level architectural judgment.

---

## User Story

**As a** Fernglen data analyst
**I want to** have near-duplicate sighting records (same species, within 50 meters, within 10 minutes) automatically merged during ingest
**So that** species counts and location reports reflect distinct observations instead of being inflated by duplicate reports of the same sighting

---

## Acceptance Criteria

- [ ] Given two sighting records of the same species reported within 50 meters and within 10 minutes of each other, `dedupe_nearby_sightings()` merges them into a single sighting record.
- [ ] Given two sighting records of different species (any distance/time), `dedupe_nearby_sightings()` does NOT merge them.
- [ ] Given two sighting records of the same species more than 50 meters apart (even within 10 minutes), `dedupe_nearby_sightings()` does NOT merge them.
- [ ] Given two sighting records of the same species within 50 meters but more than 10 minutes apart, `dedupe_nearby_sightings()` does NOT merge them.
- [ ] Boundary values (exactly 50 meters, exactly 10 minutes) have explicit, documented, and tested behavior (inclusive or exclusive — decided during implementation and reflected in both code and tests).
- [ ] The merged record's field-resolution rule (e.g., which timestamp, observer, or notes field is retained) is documented in code and covered by a test.
- [ ] All existing tests in `ingest/test_dedupe.py` continue to pass unmodified.
- [ ] New unit tests are added to `ingest/test_dedupe.py` covering each case above (merge, species mismatch, distance miss, time miss, boundary).

---

